---
import { repoUrl, subLegend, pushToTalk } from '../data/site'

/**
 * The first viewport's argument, made as an image before a word is read: a whole
 * spectrum burning across every frequency, and one column in it that carries no
 * signal at all. That silent column is your machine.
 *
 * The headline is authored as three lines rather than wrapped, because the
 * display face is synthetically compressed and compression must never interact
 * with line breaking — a reflow would change the ink width of every line at
 * once. Each line is its own `.hl-line` block.
 */
const headline = ['Speech to text', 'local by default', 'connected by choice']
---

<section class="hero relative isolate overflow-hidden" aria-labelledby="hero-headline">
  {
    /*
      1536x617 at 2.489:1, cropped from the generated plate to exactly the
      band's aspect so `cover` has almost nothing to trim on a desktop viewport.
      That matters because the scarlet lance below is drawn in CSS and must
      register to the void column inside this raster: a plate composed at the
      wrong aspect drifts the column out from under the lance.

      The image is an abstract spectrogram, not a photograph of anything, and
      the argument is carried by one feature of it — a single perfectly black
      column at the centre of a field that is otherwise blazing. Measured off the
      delivered plate: the void spans x 738-787 of 1536 (centre 49.64%) and is
      contiguously black from the top edge down to row 299 of 617 (48.5%), where
      the spectrum reclaims it. The whole page says local execution is the
      default; this is that default as a picture — everywhere signal,
      and one channel that stays silent until the user chooses a network path. If the plate is regenerated, those three
      numbers have to be re-measured with it.

      eager + high priority and no lazy attribute: this is the LCP element, and
      an above-the-fold hero that fades in late is a slower page pretending to
      be a considered one.
    */
  }
  <img
    src="/images/hero-spectrum.webp"
    width="1536"
    height="617"
    alt="An abstract spectrogram: hundreds of fine vertical frequency bins burning in magenta and cyan on black, densest at the right and dissolving to darkness at the left, over a solid rail of low-frequency light along the bottom. One column at the centre is perfectly black, carrying no signal at all."
    class="absolute inset-0 -z-20 h-full w-full object-cover"
    fetchpriority="high"
  />

  {
    /*
      Scrim, and only below lg. The plate earns its type zone by construction:
      the spectrum dissolves leftward, so the block the headline occupies at lg
      (x 2.86–55%) measures 0.4% median luminance and 19.2% at p95 — the type
      sits on the plate unassisted, no scrim required. Below lg the plate is
      cover-cropped hard toward its dense, bright right side, which is exactly
      the region the desktop composition keeps the type out of, so there the
      type needs a graded ground. A flat neutral ramp, not a glow behind the
      words: this world has no bloom under any text.
    */
  }
  <div
    class="from-night/95 via-night/70 absolute inset-0 -z-10 bg-gradient-to-t to-transparent lg:hidden"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
  </div>

  {
    /*
      The lance. Not baked into the raster, because it animates, and because a
      mark painted into the plate cannot be turned off for reduced motion.

      It runs down the inside of the void column: `left: 49.64%` is that column's
      measured centre and `height: 48.5%` is exactly its contiguous black run, so
      the lance stops on the row where the spectrum closes over it. Scarlet is
      the interface accent and this is the only place it touches the imagery,
      which is the point — the page's one accent colour marking the page's one
      silent channel, and stopping precisely where the silence does.

      A 2px scarlet line is invisible over a lit spectrogram and unmissable
      inside a black column, so the composition does the work that a scrim would
      otherwise have to.
    */
  }
  <div
    class="lance pointer-events-none absolute top-0 -z-10 hidden w-0.5 sm:block"
    style="left: 49.64%; height: 48.5%;"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
  </div>

  {
    /*
      Three flaring bins and the drift, and that is the whole motion budget for
      the first viewport. The previous world's answer to "make it feel alive" was a
      waveform and a signal meter animating numbers the product had never
      measured, which is the thing this rebuild exists to remove. So the motion
      here does nothing but light: it makes no claim, reports no value, and
      carries no data.

      Each lamp is a soft radial gradient in `screen` blend mode, placed over a
      bin cluster measured in the plate — 69.8%/8%, 85.5%/30% and 55%/26%, at
      29.4%, 32.1% and 19.1% mean luminance. Screen over bins that are already
      burning reads as those bins surging; a rectangle or a hard-edged div over
      the plate would read as a div over a plate. Gradients also have no edge to
      misregister, which is why they can be placed approximately where the lance
      cannot. Their colours follow the plate's own two pigments: magenta at
      69.8% and 55%, cyan at 85.5%.

      All of it is decorative and all of it is behind the type.
    */
  }
  <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 hidden lg:block" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="lamp lamp-a"></span>
    <span class="lamp lamp-b"></span>
    <span class="lamp lamp-c"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="drift pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 overflow-hidden" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="drift-near"></span>
    <span class="drift-far"></span>
  </div>

  <div class="hero-content">
    <div class="max-w-[92%] lg:max-w-none">
      <h1 id="hero-headline" class="display-hero text-ink">
        {headline.map((line) => <span class="hl-line">{line}</span>)}
      </h1>

      {
        /*
          The comp sets this legend on one line, and one line of it is 638px —
          which runs 158px past the edge of the plate's dark zone and puts the
          words "and the silicon" over the road's specular reflections, measured
          at 2.17% of pixels above 40% luminance against 0% under the rest of the
          line. So the measure is 40ch, chosen so the wrap lands exactly on the
          sentence boundary rather than mid-clause: two claims, two lines. The
          comp loses this one to the plate it was drawn against.
        */
      }
      <p class="mono-legend text-ink mt-7 max-w-[40ch] tracking-[0.02em] uppercase">
        {subLegend}
      </p>

      {
        /*
          The comp sets the caption beside the button. It cannot go there: its
          right edge is a fixed ~547px from the content's left edge, and the
          narrower the viewport the further right that pixel maps into the
          raster — at 1280 it reaches raster x 681 at 97% of the band height,
          which is the brightest wet asphalt in the frame, and the words stop
          being readable. Stacked, it starts at the content's left edge and
          never leaves the unlit left wall at any width.

          Stacking also lets it precede the button, which is the better order
          anyway: the caveat is what a reader needs before the click, not after
          it. This is the second thing the plate has taken off the comp, after
          the sub-legend's measure.
        */
      }
      <div class="mt-7 flex flex-col items-start gap-y-6">
        <p class="mono-caption text-ink-soft uppercase">
          Linux releases published · source available.
        </p>
        <a class="cta-solid" href={repoUrl} rel="noopener">Read the source</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {
    /*
      The one rounded object in the world, and the only element here that claims
      to be a physical thing rather than printed matter: radius, an inner bevel,
      an outer shadow and eight domed rivets, none of which any other element on
      this page is permitted. It carries the real default shortcut, so it stays
      live text.
    */
  }
  <div class="hero-plate rivet-plate text-center">
    <p class="mono-micro text-ink-soft mt-6 uppercase">{pushToTalk.above}</p>
    <p class="display-plate text-scarlet-lamp mt-1">{pushToTalk.key}</p>
    <p class="mono-micro text-ink-soft mt-1 uppercase">{pushToTalk.below}</p>
    {
      /*
        The note is not decoration and not an afterthought: without it the plate
        asserts F2 as though the product had one shortcut, when F2 is a default
        the user can rebind and the hold is a mode they can switch to a toggle.
        `site.ts` already recorded that the page says "default" — this line is
        the page keeping that promise. Hairline above it because it is a footnote
        to the key, not a fourth peer label.
      */
    }
    <p class="mono-caption text-ink-soft border-rule mx-4 mt-3 mb-7 border-t pt-2.5 normal-case">
      {pushToTalk.note}
    </p>
    <div class="chevron-strip absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-1.5 rounded-t-[9px]" aria-hidden="true">
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /*
     Comp geometry, held as a ratio rather than a pixel height. The plate is
     2.489:1, so at a desktop viewport the band's own aspect is the right height
     and `cover` trims nothing; the vh cap keeps a wide monitor from pushing the
     mode strip below the fold, and the floor keeps a short laptop from
     flattening the spectrum into a letterbox slot — the plate's whole argument
     is vertical bins, and a band too short to give them height destroys it.

     `min-height`, not `height`, and a flex column anchored to the bottom. Below
     lg the type and the plate are in the flow, and at 390px they are 40px taller
     than the 26rem floor — with a fixed height and `justify-content: flex-end`
     that surplus is pushed off the *top* of an `overflow-hidden` band, which
     decapitates the headline. A floor lets the band grow instead.
  */
  .hero {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    min-height: clamp(26rem, min(100vw / 2.489, 66svh), 46rem);
  }

  /* Below lg the composition changes rather than shrinking: the type stacks at
     the foot of the frame over the scrim, and the spectrum with its one silent
     column reads above it. The top padding is what guarantees the spectrum keeps
     a band of its own even when the content grows past the floor. */
  .hero-content {
    position: relative;
    padding: 4rem 1.25rem 1.75rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .hero-content {
      padding: 4rem 2rem 1.75rem;
    }
  }

  /*
     At lg the block leaves the flow and is placed on the plate: left edge
     at 2.86% of the viewport, stopping 24% short of the right so it never runs
     under the plate.

     Vertically centred rather than pinned to the comp's 15.6%. That pin is a
     1536px measurement, and the block does not scale with the band: the
     headline is sized in vw, but the legend, the caveat and the button are in
     rem, so the block runs 83% of the band's height at 1536 and 85% at 1440. A
     15.6% top there puts its foot 4px through the bottom edge of an
     `overflow-hidden` section, and 23px through at 1280. The pin survives only
     above about 1500px, which is too narrow a window to hold a breakpoint.
     Centring is stable at every width and costs 43px of upward shift at 1536.
  */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    /* A higher floor once the block is on the plate. The aspect term only
       overtakes 30rem at about 1195px wide, and below that the band would be
       417px tall holding a 410px block — the type would run edge to edge with
       nothing of the spectrum left above or below it. */
    .hero {
      min-height: clamp(30rem, min(100vw / 2.489, 66svh), 46rem);
    }

    .hero-content {
      position: absolute;
      top: 50%;
      translate: 0 -50%;
      left: 2.86%;
      right: 24%;
      padding: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Below lg the plate is in the flow under the CTA. It cannot be absolute
     there: the flowed content already fills the band at those widths, so a
     bottom-right plate lands on the caption and a top-right one lands on the
     headline. Flowed, it is also finally present at 390px, where the previous
     `hidden sm:block` dropped the product's one concrete affordance from the
     phone entirely. */
  .hero-plate {
    position: relative;
    width: 15.5rem;
    max-width: calc(100% - 2.5rem);
    margin: 0 1.25rem 2.5rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .hero-plate {
      margin: 0 2rem 2.5rem;
    }
  }

  /* The hazard strip occupies the plate's top edge, so the upper rivet row has
     to sit below it rather than under it. Only this instance of the plate has a
     strip, so the offset is local rather than baked into the component. */
  .hero-plate::before {
    top: 0.875rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .hero-plate {
      position: absolute;
      right: 1.9%;
      bottom: 2.3%;
      width: 16.5%;
      min-width: 13.25rem;
      max-width: none;
      margin: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Nothing at the top, full pigment at the foot, where the void column ends
     and the spectrum takes over. The pulse is 6s and moves opacity only — a
     lance that lengthened or swept would be a screensaver, and one that swept
     would also be pretending to scan something. */
  .lance {
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      transparent 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-scarlet) 45%, transparent) 35%,
      var(--color-scarlet) 88%,
      var(--color-scarlet-lamp) 100%
    );
    animation: lance-breathe 6s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes lance-breathe {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.55;
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  /* ---- Flaring bins ----------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Three soft glows sitting on three of the plate's brightest clusters, so what
     flickers is energy the raster already has rather than a light source the
     image never contained. Positions are measured off the delivered plate: each
     region below carries a mean luminance of 47-82 out of 255, which is bright
     enough that a screen-blended glow reads as a bin flaring and not as a stain
     on empty black. All three sit right of the void column and none touches it —
     the one thing on this plate that must never appear to emit anything. */
  .lamp {
    position: absolute;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .lamp-a {
    left: 69.8%;
    top: 8%;
    width: 13%;
    height: 20%;
    background-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, var(--sign-magenta) 0%, transparent 70%);
    /* Energy in a real spectrum does not swell and fade on a sine. It holds,
       stumbles, double-blinks and holds again, so the keyframes are deliberately
       uneven and the three share no period — 7s, 5.4s and 11s never line up into
       a visible pulse. */
    animation: flicker-a 7s steps(1, end) infinite;
  }

  .lamp-b {
    left: 85.5%;
    top: 30%;
    width: 12%;
    height: 20%;
    background-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, var(--sign-cyan) 0%, transparent 70%);
    animation: flicker-b 5.4s steps(1, end) infinite;
  }

  .lamp-c {
    left: 55%;
    top: 26%;
    width: 12%;
    height: 20%;
    background-image: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, var(--sign-magenta) 0%, transparent 70%);
    animation: flicker-c 11s steps(1, end) infinite;
  }

  @keyframes flicker-a {
    0%,
    44%,
    47%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.18;
    }
    45% {
      opacity: 0.5;
    }
    46% {
      opacity: 0.06;
    }
    72%,
    74% {
      opacity: 0.44;
    }
    73% {
      opacity: 0.1;
    }
  }

  @keyframes flicker-b {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.22;
    }
    26% {
      opacity: 0.4;
    }
    27%,
    29% {
      opacity: 0.08;
    }
    28% {
      opacity: 0.36;
    }
  }

  @keyframes flicker-c {
    0%,
    62%,
    100% {
      opacity: 0.14;
    }
    63% {
      opacity: 0.05;
    }
    64% {
      opacity: 0.32;
    }
    65% {
      opacity: 0.12;
    }
  }

  /* ---- Drift ------------------------------------------------------------ */
  /* The previous world's atmosphere here was falling rain, which belonged to a
     wet street and belongs to nothing in a spectrogram — nothing in this plate
     falls. What a spectrogram does instead is advance: the field slides as time
     passes. So the two streak layers survive as two layers of vertical striation
     travelling sideways, which is the same restraint doing the same job in the
     new world's own idiom.

     Two scales at two speeds, so the near layer moves visibly faster than the
     far one and the frame gains depth rather than a single moving texture. Each
     layer is 200% *wide* and travels exactly 50% of its own width, so the loop
     has no seam.

     90deg exactly, not the rain's 100deg. The angle in
     `repeating-linear-gradient` orients the gradient *axis* and the bands run
     perpendicular to it, so 90deg gives bands precisely plumb. The rain leaned
     10deg off vertical because falling water does; frequency bins do not lean,
     and a raked striation over an upright spectrum reads as a printing error. */
  .drift-near,
  .drift-far {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 -50%;
    width: 200%;
    background-repeat: repeat;
  }

  .drift-near {
    background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      transparent 0 9px,
      rgb(255 255 255 / 0.055) 9px 10px,
      transparent 10px 22px
    );
    animation: drift-scan 0.75s linear infinite;
  }

  .drift-far {
    background-image: repeating-linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      transparent 0 15px,
      rgb(255 255 255 / 0.03) 15px 16px,
      transparent 16px 40px
    );
    animation: drift-scan 1.9s linear infinite;
  }

  /* Leftward, because a spectrogram's newest column arrives at the right edge
     and the history slides away from it. */
  @keyframes drift-scan {
    from {
      transform: translate3d(25%, 0, 0);
    }
    to {
      transform: translate3d(-25%, 0, 0);
    }
  }

  /*
     Reduced motion needs a rendering, not a freeze. The global rule neutralises
     every duration, which would leave the drift as a static hatch of vertical
     lines ruled across the spectrum and the lamps stuck at whatever opacity
     their first keyframe happens to hold — both worse than the plate on its own.
     So the drift is removed outright, the lamps are pinned to a plausible steady
     glow, and the lance simply stops breathing at full strength, since it is a
     composition element and not an effect.
  */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .drift {
      display: none;
    }

    .lance {
      opacity: 1;
    }

    .lamp-a,
    .lamp-b,
    .lamp-c {
      animation: none;
      opacity: 0.2;
    }
  }
</style>
